        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$^{n}C_{2}-n=\\dfrac{n(n-3)}{2}$",
          "worked": "Choosing two vertices counts every side and diagonal once. Subtracting the $n$ sides gives $^{n}C_{2}-n$ diagonals.\n\nAlternatively, each of the $n$ vertices connects by a diagonal to $n-3$ vertices: exclude itself and its two neighbours. This counts each diagonal twice, giving $\\dfrac{n(n-3)}{2}$ diagonals.\n\nBoth methods count the same diagonals, giving the combinatorial argument\n$$^{n}C_{2}-n=\\frac{n(n-3)}{2}.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 24
      }
